================================================================================
PyAV and the FFmpeg libraries it bundles (used by faster-whisper)
================================================================================
The app's Python engine includes PyAV, which faster-whisper uses to decode
audio. PyAV runs inside the engine's Python process.

  PyAV 18.1.0 (BSD-3-Clause)  https://github.com/PyAV-Org/PyAV/tree/v18.1.0
                              https://pypi.org/project/av/18.1.0/
  faster-whisper (MIT)        https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper

The PyAV 18.1.0 wheels bundle shared FFmpeg libraries, built by pyav-ffmpeg:
  FFmpeg 8.1.2 (libavutil 60.26.102, libavcodec 62.28.102, libavformat 62.12.102,
  libavdevice 62.3.102, libavfilter 11.14.102, libswscale 9.5.102,
  libswresample 6.3.102); these are the values `av.library_versions` reports.
  The libraries report "LGPL version 3 or later" (LGPL-3.0-or-later; full text
  in licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt, which builds on licenses/FFmpeg-GPL.txt).
  The same wheels also bundle libx264 and libx265, which are licensed
  GPL-2.0-or-later. Treat that bundle as GPL-covered: the GPL text is in
  licenses/FFmpeg-GPL.txt.

Corresponding source:
  FFmpeg 8.1.2:      https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-8.1.2.tar.xz
  pyav-ffmpeg build: https://github.com/PyAV-Org/pyav-ffmpeg/tree/8.1.2-1
                     (build scripts, patches and the pinned source of every
                     bundled library; binaries at
                     https://github.com/PyAV-Org/pyav-ffmpeg/releases/tag/8.1.2-1)
  Starting with v0.5.3, every release also attaches the PyAV 18.1.0 source, the
  pyav-ffmpeg 8.1.2-1 scripts and patches, and the source of every library in
  the wheels' av.libs/ folder. See "Mirrored source archives" below.

================================================================================
OpenCV (opencv-python-headless 5.0.0.93, Apache-2.0) and its FFmpeg libraries
================================================================================
The app's engine installs the [reframe] extra. That wheel bundles OpenCV
(Apache-2.0) and shared FFmpeg libraries (libavcodec 62.28.101, libavformat
62.12.101, libavutil 60.26.101, libswscale 9.5.101) that report "LGPL version 2.1
or later" (full text in licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt). Third-party notices ship inside
the wheel (cv2/LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt).
  Source: https://github.com/opencv/opencv-python/tree/93
          https://pypi.org/project/opencv-python-headless/5.0.0.93/

The versions of PyAV and opencv-python-headless above are pinned for the app
build in packaging/engine-constraints.txt. Change this NOTICE whenever that file or the
FFMPEG_* pins in .github/workflows/desktop.yml change; tests/test_notices.py
fails otherwise.

================================================================================
Mirrored source archives (attached to every release, starting with v0.5.3)
================================================================================
Every Hermes Studio release (from v0.5.3) attaches each archive listed below as
a release asset under the file name shown, with its sha256 also in
SHA256SUMS.txt. Together they are the corresponding source of the FFmpeg
programs and the PyAV libraries bundled in the app, at the exact versions used:

  * ffmpeg-linux64 / ffmpeg-win64: everything BtbN FFmpeg-Builds
    autobuild-2026-09-30-13-08 builds into the "gpl" variant with addin 9.0
    for that target. That covers each library script with its pinned commit, the
    submodules and sub-downloads those scripts fetch that are compiled in, the
    Rust crates locked by rav1e and librsvg, and GCC (whose libstdc++, libgcc and
    libgomp are statically linked). FFmpeg itself is the commit above.
  * pyav-linux / pyav-win: the PyAV 18.1.0 sdist, pyav-ffmpeg 8.1.2-1 and every
    source tarball its scripts/pkg.py pins except the nasm assembler (same
    sha256 values), plus the
    libraries the wheel tools added: AlmaLinux 8 source RPMs for libxcb, libXau
    and libdrm (manylinux_2_28), and MSYS2 source packages for the GCC runtime
    and libiconv in the Windows wheel.

The list is in packaging/third-party-sources.txt. scripts/mirror-sources.py
rebuilds and verifies every archive. "git archive" entries come from forges
that have no stable commit tarball. "svn export" and "cargo vendor" entries are
packed as normalized tar files, so anyone can rebuild them byte for byte.

Not mirrored:
  * Submodules that the build scripts fetch but never compile in: OpenSSL's
    test, fuzz and interop suites, googletest, munit, libjxl and libplacebo
    test/demo data, and jinja/markupsafe (code generators).
  * Build tools that are not linked: nasm, cmake, meson, crosstool-NG, rustc.
    The Rust standard library from the nightly toolchain BtbN used
    (rustc 1.101.0-nightly d080e7dff, 2026-09-27) is compiled into rav1e and
    librsvg, but its exact source archive is not mirrored here.
  * rav1e: BtbN runs `cargo update cc` before building. cc is a build-script
    helper that is not linked, so the mirrored crates follow rav1e's Cargo.lock
    as committed.
  * libwinpthread-1.dll in the PyAV Windows wheel (MIT AND BSD-3-Clause): the
    DLL carries no version, so the exact MSYS2 winpthreads package cannot be
    identified.
  * zlib1.dll in the PyAV Windows wheel is zlib 1.3.2. The upstream 1.3.2
    tarball is mirrored, but which MSYS2 package release (1.3.2-1 or 1.3.2-2)
    built it cannot be told from the DLL.
  * The FFmpeg libraries inside the opencv-python-headless wheel
    (LGPL-2.1-or-later) are covered by the opencv-python source link above. They
    are not mirrored here.
  * The Linux ffmpeg links the system's glibc and libgcc_s at run time. Those
    are not shipped with the app.

